bearing hoppers; a subfamily or other division of the great family ''Cercopidæ'', represented by the genera ''Aphrophora'', ''Lepyronia'', ''Ptyelus'', and many others, and containing a great many species of medium or small size, very generally distributed over the world, and especially affecting pines and willows.

'''aphrosiderite''' (af-rō-sid′e̤-rīt), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀφρός, foam, + σίδηρος, iron, + ''-ite''<sup>2</sup>.] A ferruginous chloritic mineral occurring in soft dark-green scales.

'''aphtha''' (af′thä̤), ''n.''; pl. ''aphthæ'' (-thē). [NL., < L. ''aphthæ'', pl., < Gr. ἄφθαι, pl. of ἄφθα, an eruption, ulceration, < ἅπτειν, set on fire, inflame.] In ''pathol.'', an eruption; an ulceration: used especially in the plural to denote small round ulcers, sometimes becoming confluent, and said in some cases to be preceded by vesicles which break. {{smaller|They occur upon the tongue, gums, inside of the lips, and palate. When ''Mycoderma vini'' (''Oidium albicans'') is found in these ulcers, the disease is called ''thrush'', or ''milk-thrush''. Also ''aptha''.—'''Aphthæ epizoöticæ''', foot-and-mouth disease (which see, under ''foot'').}}

'''aphthalose''' (af′tha-lōs), ''n.'' [As ''aphth''(''it'')''al''(''ite'') + ''-ose''.] Same as ''aphthitalite''.

'''Aphthartodocetæ''' (af-thär″tō-dō-sē′tē), ''n. pl.'' [< LGr. *Ἀφθαρτοδοκῆται, < Gr. ἄφθαρτος, uncorrupted, incorruptible (< ἀ- priv. + φθαρτός, verbal adj. of φθείρειν, destroy, ruin, corrupt), + δοκεῖν, think. Cf. ''Docetæ''.] A Monophysite sect which existed from the sixth to the ninth century, or later. {{smaller|They held that the body of Christ was incorruptible even before the resurrection, and that he suffered death only in a phantasmal appearance. From this they are sometimes called ''Phantasiasts'', a name more properly belonging to the Docetæ (which see), who denied even the}}
